## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of provider aliases and per-environment module instantiation in Terraform.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases and module-level provider mappings
- For_each environment deployment and target verification
- Safe plan/apply separation per environment

## Code Example
```javascript
provider "aws" {
  alias = \"dev\"
  region = \"us-west-2\"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias = \"prod\"
  region = \"us-east-1\"
}
variable \"environments\" { type = \"map(string)\"; default = { prod = \"prod\"; staging = \"staging\" } }

module \"vpc\" {
  for_each = var.environments
  source   = \"./modules/vpc\"
  providers = {
    aws = each.key == \"prod\" ? aws.prod : aws.dev
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback if prod plan uses the wrong alias?
- How can you enforce alias usage at plan time to prevent mix-ups?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement policy-as-code governance for Terraform Cloud, ensuring prod safety beyond backend controls.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform Cloud and Sentinel governance
- Env-to-workspace mapping and state linkage
- CI integration with GitHub Actions for plan/apply gating
- Policy design distinguishing prod vs non-prod changes
- Local and CI testing of policies; post-merge audit of policy outcomes

## Code Example

```sentinel
import "tfplan/v2"

check = func(plan) {
  hasProdChange = false
  for _, rc in tfplan.resource_changes {
    if strings.contains(rc.address, "envs/prod/") {
      hasProdChange = true
      break
    }
  }
  // If prod resources are touched, deny unless approved in PR metadata
  if hasProdChange {
    return false
  }
  return true
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evolve the policy to support time-bound approvals or multi-degree approvals?
- How would you validate policy effectiveness across 100s of PRs and detect gaps in coverage?
